digraph ReasoningTree {
  // paper_id=s41467-025-56877-9
  rankdir="TB";
  "N1" [label="(1,0,0) Currently, harsh environments (e.g., marine) increase the demand for higher-durability concrete that resists ion corrosion and dissolution degradation."];
  "N10C" [label="(0,0,0) deduction-reasoning: Given currently SCAs have alkoxy groups, and if alkoxy groups hydrolyze in acidic/alkaline solutions to form –OH, therefore SCA alkoxy groups hydrolyze to form hydroxyl groups."];
  "N14" [label="(8,0,0) Currently, silane in concrete is applied via two approaches: surface treatment and initial admixture."];
  "N15" [label="(9,0,0) Currently, in surface treatment, hydrolyzed silane monomers diffuse into concrete through capillary pores driven by a concentration gradient."];
  "N18" [label="(12,0,0) Currently, the highly alkaline cement environment facilitates silane hydrolysis, leading to C–S–H condensation and Si–O–Si network formation."];
  "N2" [label="(2,0,0) Currently, in harsh service environments, improving concrete surface hydrophobicity is crucial to protect substrates from water infiltration."];
  "N21" [label="(14,0,0) Currently, APTES incorporation can greatly improve cement paste fluidity and induce acicular hydration products that improve microstructure."];
  "N22" [label="(15,0,0) Currently, reports confirm silane modification improves flexural performance of cement paste."];
  "N24" [label="(0,0,0) If multiple independent studies report improved workability or microstructure or strength from silane/APTES, then it is reasonable to generalize that SCAs can provide performance benefits beyond hydrophobicity."];
  "N26" [label="(17,0,0) Currently, despite benefits, including SCAs can adversely inhibit initial cement hydration."];
  "N27" [label="(18,0,0) Currently, incorporating SCAs significantly delays the hydration peak, thereby inhibiting initial hydration."];
  "N28" [label="(19,0,0) Currently, one plausible explanation is that hydrophobic silane groups increase repulsive forces between free water and cement particles, inhibiting initial hydration."];
  "N29" [label="(20,0,0) Currently, another study confirms inhibitory effects on hydration and indicates hydrolysis byproducts (e.g., alcohols) have negligible influence."];
  "N3" [label="(0,0,0) If water infiltration into concrete is reduced, then ion ingress and dissolution-related degradation are mitigated, improving durability in harsh environments."];
  "N30" [label="(21,0,0) Currently, hydrophobic groups can negatively impact silicate polymerization and hydrogen-bond formation, affecting C–S–H nucleation and retarding hydration."];
  "N31" [label="(22,0,0) Currently, incorporating silane reduces C–S–H quantity and cumulative density, hindering hydration."];
  "N32" [label="(23,0,0) Currently, silane hydrolysates can complex with Ca2+, potentially accounting for delayed hydration."];
  "N33" [label="(24,0,0) Currently, there are still gaps in understanding the mechanisms of silane-induced delayed hydration."];
  "N34" [label="(25,0,0) Currently, hydration is complex/prolonged, and silane may have different retarding mechanisms at different stages (even if existing hypotheses are plausible)."];
  "N35" [label="(26,0,0) Currently, complexation/inhibition of C–S–H growth appears more relevant to the post-induction stage."];
  "N36" [label="(27,0,0) Currently, the precise impact of silane on clinker dissolution during the induction stage warrants further exploration."];
  "N36B" [label="(0,0,0) deduction-reasoning: Given currently complexation/C–S–H-growth inhibition mainly explains post-induction retardation, and if post-induction relevance implies induction-stage retardation needs additional investigation, therefore induction-stage retardation likely requires further study (e.g., effects on clinker dissolution)."];
  "N37" [label="(28,0,0) Currently, mineral dissolution occurs within seconds, so direct observation is very challenging."];
  "N38B" [label="(0,0,0) deduction-reasoning: Given currently dissolution occurs within seconds and is hard to observe, and if fast dissolution is hard to observe directly implying simulations can access atomic-scale events, therefore atomic-scale simulations are justified for studying induction-stage dissolution."];
  "N39" [label="(30,0,0) Currently, atomistic simulations provide valuable nanoscale insight into how SCAs inhibit cement hydration."];
  "N4" [label="(0,0,0) deduction-reasoning: Given currently durability against ion corrosion/dissolution is needed, and if reducing water infiltration mitigates such degradation, therefore increasing hydrophobicity/impermeability is a key durability route in harsh environments."];
  "N40" [label="(36,0,0) Currently, AIMD is increasingly adopted to accurately probe chemical reactions (e.g., adsorption/nucleation) in cement hydration."];
  "N43" [label="(0,0,0) If induction-stage retardation may involve effects on clinker dissolution and direct experiments are hard, then a suitable research strategy is to use AIMD+MetaD (validated by DFT where needed) plus experiments to connect atomistic dissolution changes to measurable hydration kinetics."];
  "N44" [label="(0,0,0) deduction-reasoning: Given currently induction-stage silane impact on clinker dissolution is an open gap, and if AIMD+MetaD plus experiments can resolve fast dissolution mechanisms, therefore the research should target silane effects on early clinker (Ca3SiO5) dissolution using AIMD+MetaD with experimental corroboration."];
  "N45" [label="(39,0,0) Currently, the proposed study uses ab initio simulations plus experimental corroboration to investigate how APTES affects early Ca3SiO5 hydration at the nanoscale to elucidate SCA retardation."];
  "N46" [label="(0,0,0) If SCAs are desirable for durability/performance but may retard early hydration via an unclear induction-stage mechanism, then a focused proposal is to quantify how a representative SCA (APTES) alters Ca dissolution from Ca3SiO5 and relate that to early hydration kinetics using integrated ab initio simulation and experiments."];
  "N47" [label="(0,0,0) deduction-reasoning: Given currently SCAs/APTES are promising for durable, workable cementitious materials yet can delay early hydration with unclear induction-stage dissolution effects, and if integrated AIMD/MetaD(+DFT validation) plus experiments can reveal how APTES changes Ca3SiO5 Ca-ion dissolution and thus early hydration kinetics, therefore the core research proposal is to study APTES-induced retardation by atomistically modeling Ca dissolution from Ca3SiO5 during induction and experimentally corroborating hydration kinetics."];
  "N47SUP" [label="(0,0,0) deduction-reasoning: Given currently SCAs are motivated by durability/performance but early hydration retardation and induction-stage dissolution effects are uncertain, and if a mechanistic multi-scale approach can resolve APTES effects on Ca3SiO5 dissolution and early hydration, therefore an integrated AIMD+MetaD+DFT validation plus experiments approach is warranted."];
  "N48" [label="(40,0,0) Currently, the methodological plan includes validating the MetaD sampling model via DFT adsorption tests and then exploring dissolution of surface penta-coordinated Ca under APTES using ab initio MetaD."];
  "N49" [label="(0,0,0) If MetaD is used to study dissolution pathways/free energies, then validating the sampling/interaction description via DFT adsorption benchmarks increases credibility of the AIMD+MetaD dissolution predictions."];
  "N4B" [label="(0,0,0) deduction-reasoning: Given currently hydrophobicity is crucial for preventing water infiltration, and if hydrophobicity/blocked pore penetration improves impermeability and protection, therefore hydrophobicity is a key mechanism to pursue for durability."];
  "N5" [label="(3,0,0) Currently, incorporating silane coupling agents (SCAs) is an effective strategy to improve concrete impermeability."];
  "N50" [label="(0,0,0) deduction-reasoning: Given currently the plan validates MetaD with DFT adsorption tests, and if such validation improves credibility of dissolution simulations, therefore the study design appropriately integrates DFT validation with AIMD+MetaD dissolution modeling."];
  "N54" [label="(0,0,0) abduction-reasoning: Given currently SCAs delay hydration (phenomenon) and if altered clinker dissolution or Ca2+ availability could cause such delay, therefore hypothesize that during the induction stage APTES shields Ca3SiO5 surface Ca sites and reduces Ca2+ release, contributing to delayed hydration."];
  "N55" [label="(0,0,0) deduction-reasoning: Given currently SCAs have alkoxy-containing silane structures, and if alkoxy-containing silanes hydrolyze in acidic/alkaline solutions to form –OH, therefore SCAs can hydrolyze to form hydroxyl groups."];
  "N56" [label="(0,0,0) If a molecule contains alkoxy groups (–RO)3 on Si, then in acidic/alkaline solutions it can hydrolyze to form –OH groups."];
  "N58" [label="(0,0,0) If hydroxyl groups are present on hydrolyzed silanes, then they can participate in condensation reactions with C–S–H to form siloxane linkages."];
  "N59" [label="(0,0,0) deduction-reasoning: Given currently silane application includes surface treatment and initial admixture, and if initial admixture yields more uniform distribution than diffusion-based surface treatment, therefore initial admixture is positioned as a potentially more consistent application route."];
  "N6" [label="(7,0,0) Currently, SCAs provide organo-functional groups that impart hydrophobicity and effectively prevent water from penetrating concrete pores."];
  "N60" [label="(0,0,0) If a technology has a diffusion-limited route and an integrated mixing route, then the integrated mixing route can yield more uniform distribution."];
  "N62" [label="(0,0,0) If transport into a porous solid relies on concentration-gradient diffusion through capillary pores, then penetration depth and uniformity can be limited by diffusion."];
  "N63" [label="(0,0,0) deduction-reasoning: Given currently alkalinity facilitates silane hydrolysis leading to C–S–H condensation and Si–O–Si networks, and if alkaline-promoted hydrolysis/condensation strengthens network formation, therefore cement alkalinity supports effective silane network formation in admixture."];
  "N64" [label="(0,0,0) If the cement pore solution is highly alkaline, then silane hydrolysis and subsequent condensation/network formation are facilitated."];
  "N65" [label="(0,0,0) induction-reasoning: Given currently SCAs inhibit initial hydration and hydration-peak delay is observed, therefore early-age hydration retardation is a recurring concern when using SCAs."];
  "N66" [label="(0,0,0) abduction-reasoning: Given currently initial hydration is inhibited, and if hydrophobic groups can increase repulsive forces between water and cement particles reducing effective wetting, therefore increased repulsion due to hydrophobic silane groups is a plausible explanation for early hydration inhibition."];
  "N67" [label="(0,0,0) deduction-reasoning: Given currently inhibitory effects are observed and byproduct influence is negligible, and if the byproducts are negligible then inhibition is more likely attributable to the silane/hydrophobic-group interactions, therefore focus should be on silane-surface/water interactions rather than alcohol byproducts."];
  "N68" [label="(0,0,0) If hydrolysis byproducts have negligible influence on hydration, then observed hydration inhibition should be attributed primarily to other aspects of the additive such as hydrophobic group interactions or adsorption/complexation."];
  "N69" [label="(0,0,0) deduction-reasoning: Given currently hydrophobic groups negatively impact silicate polymerization and hydrogen bonding, and if disrupting polymerization/hydrogen bonds hinders C–S–H nucleation, therefore hydrophobic silane groups can retard hydration by inhibiting C–S–H nucleation."];
  "N7" [label="(0,0,0) If a treatment increases surface hydrophobicity or blocks pore water penetration, then it increases impermeability and helps protect concrete from water-driven degradation."];
  "N70" [label="(0,0,0) If silicate polymerization and hydrogen-bond formation are disrupted, then C–S–H nucleation is hindered and hydration is retarded."];
  "N71" [label="(0,0,0) deduction-reasoning: Given currently incorporating silane reduces C–S–H quantity/density, and if reduced C–S–H indicates hindered hydration product formation, therefore silane can hinder hydration by reducing C–S–H formation."];
  "N72" [label="(0,0,0) If an additive reduces the quantity or density of C–S–H, then the hydration process is hindered."];
  "N73" [label="(0,0,0) abduction-reasoning: Given currently hydration is delayed, and if silane hydrolysates can complex Ca2+ reducing available Ca2+ needed for hydration product formation, therefore Ca2+ complexation by silane hydrolysates is a plausible explanation for delayed hydration."];
  "N75" [label="(0,0,0) If a phenomenon is too fast for direct observation, then atomic-scale simulations can be an appropriate tool to study it."];
  "N76" [label="(0,0,0) deduction-reasoning: Given currently atomistic simulations provide nanoscale insight into SCA hydration inhibition, and if such insight can reveal mechanistic pathways inaccessible to experiments, therefore atomistic simulations are valuable for elucidating SCA-induced retardation mechanisms."];
  "N77" [label="(0,0,0) If a modeling method provides nanoscale mechanistic insight into inhibition processes, then it is valuable for elucidating causes of macroscopic retardation."];
  "N78" [label="(0,0,0) deduction-reasoning: Given currently AIMD can probe chemical reactions in cement hydration, and if accurate probing of electron-transfer reactions is needed for adsorption/nucleation/dissolution, therefore AIMD is suitable for studying reactive steps relevant to hydration and dissolution."];
  "N79" [label="(0,0,0) If a method can accurately probe chemical reactions involving electron transfer, then it is suitable for studying adsorption/nucleation/dissolution reactions in cement hydration."];
  "N8" [label="(0,0,0) deduction-reasoning: Given currently SCAs impart hydrophobicity preventing pore water penetration, and if hydrophobicity/blocked penetration implies increased impermeability and protection, therefore SCAs are a mechanistically plausible durability-improving approach via hydrophobicity/impermeability."];
  "N82" [label="(0,0,0) deduction-reasoning: Given currently there are mechanistic gaps and if the induction-stage dissolution impact warrants exploration, therefore a focused induction-stage dissolution mechanism study is justified."];
  "N83" [label="(0,0,0) If there are gaps in mechanistic understanding and a specific early-stage process is flagged as needing exploration, then focusing research on that process is justified."];
  "N8B" [label="(0,0,0) deduction-reasoning: Given currently SCAs improve impermeability, and if improving impermeability mitigates water-driven degradation, therefore SCAs are relevant to meeting the durability demand."];
  "N9" [label="(4,0,0) Currently, SCAs have a bifunctional structure (organic + inorganic), often written as (RO)3–Si–X."];
  "NROOT" [label="(0,0,0) This paper motivates and develops an integrated ab initio simulation and early-age hydration experimental study to evaluate how the representative silane coupling agent APTES affects Ca3SiO5 surface Ca dissolution, Ca2+ release, and initial cement hydration, while situating that aim within the broader tradeoff between SCA durability/performance benefits and SCA-induced hydration retardation.", fillcolor="lightyellow", style="filled"];
  "NROOT_COMMON" [label="(0,0,0) The retained and repaired terminal conclusions jointly motivate the paper-level research proposal."];
  "R10" [label="(0,0,0) Silane modification has reported benefits for flexural-related hardened performance, supported by flexural-performance and strength observations."];
  "R13" [label="(0,0,0) When hydroxyl groups are present on hydrolyzed silanes, they can participate in condensation with C–S–H and form siloxane linkages on particle surfaces."];
  "R15" [label="(0,0,0) Surface treatment in which hydrolyzed silane monomers diffuse through capillary pores can be limited in penetration depth and uniformity by concentration-gradient diffusion."];
  "R23" [label="(0,0,0) Observed SCA-induced hydration-peak delay, together with acknowledged mechanistic gaps, supports the need to clarify mechanisms of silane-induced delayed hydration without singling out induction-stage dissolution by itself."];
  "R26" [label="(0,0,0) Because mineral dissolution occurs within seconds and is challenging to observe directly, atomic-scale simulations can be an appropriate tool for studying rapid clinker dissolution phenomena."];
  "R29" [label="(0,0,0) For fast induction-stage dissolution processes that are difficult to observe directly, AIMD plus MetaD and experiments are a suitable strategy for constructing mechanistic insight."];
  "R30" [label="(0,0,0) Because the impact of silane on clinker dissolution during the induction stage warrants exploration, AIMD plus MetaD with experimental corroboration is a suitable research strategy for studying APTES effects on Ca3SiO5 dissolution."];
  "R38" [label="(0,0,0) Because SCAs contain alkoxy-containing silane structures, they can hydrolyze in acidic or alkaline solution to form hydroxyl groups."];
  "R39" [label="(0,0,0) SCA alkoxy-containing silane groups can hydrolyze under acidic or alkaline conditions to generate hydroxyl groups."];
  "R5" [label="(0,0,0) Hydrolyzed SCA hydroxyl groups can participate in condensation reactions with C–S–H to form siloxane linkages, rather than proving full interfacial network formation from hydrolysis alone."];
  "R7" [label="(0,0,0) Because silane can be applied either by surface treatment or by initial admixture, the integrated initial-admixture route is positioned as a way to obtain more uniform silane distribution."];
  "R8" [label="(0,0,0) Reports on APTES and silane modification provide evidence that silane-based additives can offer performance benefits beyond hydrophobicity, including fluidity, microstructural, and mechanical improvements."];
  "R9" [label="(0,0,0) Multiple silane-related reports support the narrower claim that silane modification can improve hardened mechanical performance in reported cases."];
